  "body": "Asked for by the maintainer (2026-11-13, in chat): \"Create a logotype issue as P? to be added as application icon and into the README\". Filed `P?` at his explicit request: held until he sets a priority; no agent claims, researches or re-prioritises it before that.\n\nWhat is missing: CamCraft has no logotype. The application window, the taskbar, the installer and the packaged builds show no icon of their own, and the README opens without one.\n\nWanted:\n- A logotype for CamCraft: a mark that reads at 16 px and a wordmark version for wide places.\n- As the application icon: the window and taskbar icon in the running app, the Windows executable and installer icon (`.ico`), the macOS bundle icon (`.icns`), the Linux desktop entry icon (PNG sizes and SVG), and the browser build's favicon.\n- In the README: the logotype at the top; the documentation site header can take the same file.\n\nRules:\n- Original artwork, made for this project. Nothing adapted from HSMWorks or any Autodesk material, and no likeness to their marks (Clean-Room Protocol; Assets). If it is drawn from a third-party set or font, that licence allows bundling without attribution and its text goes in `assets/ui/LICENSES.md`.\n- The source (SVG) lives in the repository under `assets/`, and the raster sizes are generated from it, so the icon is one file to change.\n- The icon is wired in, not only stored: the app shows it (Assets: \"loading the files alone isn't enough\").\n\nOpen for the maintainer: who draws it (his own artwork, a commissioned one, or an agent's SVG proposals to choose from), and colours.\n\nDone means: the logotype source is in `assets/`; the running app, the Windows executable and installer, the macOS and Linux packages and the browser build show it; the README shows it at the top; the licence position is stated in `assets/ui/LICENSES.md`; a test pins that the window icon loads.\n",
